WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.056
They had everything.

00:00:01.066 --> 00:00:02.354
They still vanished.

00:00:02.364 --> 00:00:05.375
In 1968, John Calhoun put eight mice

00:00:05.385 --> 00:00:08.062
into a pen called Universe 25.

00:00:08.072 --> 00:00:11.333
Unlimited food and water,
nesting paper, and

00:00:11.343 --> 00:00:13.970
two hundred and fifty-six
little apartments.

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:16.442
By around day five hundred and sixty,

00:00:16.452 --> 00:00:18.787
there were two thousand two hundred mice.

00:00:18.797 --> 00:00:20.586
Then births almost stopped.

00:00:20.596 --> 00:00:22.861
Some males, the beautiful ones, just ate,

00:00:22.871 --> 00:00:24.246
slept and groomed.

00:00:24.256 --> 00:00:25.741
The last pup was born about two

00:00:25.751 --> 00:00:26.751
years in.

00:00:26.764 --> 00:00:29.346
By 1973, none were left.

00:00:29.356 --> 00:00:32.254
Calhoun warned that
overpopulation itself leads to

00:00:32.264 --> 00:00:35.084
collapse, and people applied it to cities.

00:00:35.094 --> 00:00:37.216
But crowding studies in people found mixed

00:00:37.226 --> 00:00:38.229
results.

00:00:38.239 --> 00:00:40.475
Packed together isn't feeling crowded.

00:00:40.485 --> 00:00:42.850
Later researchers argued
the mice were harmed

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:45.612
by constant social contact, not lack of

00:00:45.622 --> 00:00:46.625
space.

00:00:46.635 --> 00:00:49.210
And people cope in ways mice can't.
